-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-274700" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-274700" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{19\\pi}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-274700" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-274700" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{23\\pi}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-13)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{23\\pi }{12}=\\frac{28\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-124345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-124345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{19\\pi }{12}=\\frac{20\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-124345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-124345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9