are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\pi $\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-8482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+3=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8556" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{5\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-8550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{11\\pi }{12}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-65855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{18},\\frac{7\\pi }{18},\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{13\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11\\pi }{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{17\\pi }{18}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-121558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)=-1,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(4n-1)\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5332dc810971e60b916abd9b0e55d9218eba7203ce03d9ad3b3e445125c686e9"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5\\pi}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea9c49f08bd409be7ffd561be4195f6d7ba142cfc13259e19cc3e1543ee9a3"} {"question" "q-328366" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/h